  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nThe main weather issue for the beginning of the week will be a \nresult of a building high pressure ridge across much of the western \nCONUS into the central CONUS. As the ridge builds aloft, expected \ntemperatures to steadily warm into the low to mid 90s and possibly \neven upper 90s by Monday. Humidity will be on a steady increase as \nwell, allowing heat index values to push into the 105-110 degree \nrange through Tuesday and possibly even a bit higher on Monday. This \nhas led to the issuance of a Heat Advisory for much of the area and \nit will likely be expanded by Sunday afternoon. \n\nAs the main ridge axis remains just west of the Quad State, it is \npossible that some ridge-runner type storms may develop and brush \nthrough the eastern portions of the area, most likely over southwest \nIN into the Pennyrile. That is part of the reason that the Advisory \nhasn't been expanded east with the latest update. That is expected \nto change for Monday with extreme heat taking a foothold. If the \nstorms do brush through the area, there is plenty of heat and \nhumidity (fuel) for the storms to work with, so some strong to \nsevere storms would be possible along with heavy downpours. There is \na marginal risk of severe storms Monday afternoon/evening across far \nnortheast/far eastern portions of the CWA in the aformentioned \nlocations. \n\nThe high is still expected to retrograde back to the southwest, but \nnot completely out of the area for the end of the work week, which \nwill allow temperatures and heat index values to pull back as well. \nAdditionally, a stronger wave is modeled to drop south into the Quad \nState Friday night into Saturday, while dragging a cold front \nthrough the area. The at will be the most likely (20-30% chance) \ntime for showers and storms to move into the area again. There's no \nstrong signal for severe weather during that time, but plenty of \nfuel will be around for the storms, so it is worth keeping an eye on!",
